<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fraxinetum</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 890–973</span> · Also known as Fraxinet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fraxinetum was a fortified base set up by Muslim adventurers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> around 890 near modern La Garde-Freinet, in the Maures hills of Provence. From it raiders controlled Alpine passes, plundered monasteries and in 972 captured Abbot <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majolus-of-cluny">Majolus of Cluny</a>. The outrage prompted a campaign led by William, count of Provence, and Arduin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>, who destroyed the base around 973.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fraxinetum.jpg" alt="Fraxinetum" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Enyavar · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fraxinetum_EN.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
